00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
it's election day in the united states  and today and all this week  we'll be looking at the impact of the  vote on countries across asia  one of the most important relationships  for the u.s and the indo-pacific  is its engagement with india the two  countries together with japan  and australia form part of what is known  as the quad for democracies  in a clear counterpoint to china the  region's large authoritarian state  we sat down with india's foreign  secretary harshvardhan shringla  to discuss regional politics we started  by asking him  whether there be any change with a biden  presidency especially given president  donald trump  and prime minister narendra modi's great  personal admiration for each other  our relations with the united states are  really based on  bipartisan support you see it in  congress  you see it uh you know at the public  levels  we do believe that we've forged a  relationship that today  is has withstood the test of time is uh  is very comprehensive and multifaceted  we not only share the same values and  principles but we also  i think have the same strategic vision  in what  could be a bilateral regional or  multilateral  relationship now prime minister modi's  relationship with president trump have  been special  but you have to remember that prime  minister modi's relationship with  president obama was also very special  i remember that state dinner that  president obama held  am i correct in in hearing that you  believe that regardless  of who wins the presidency in the united  states the relationship will  fundamentally not change  vice president biden who is  the democratic candidate has also made  it very clear in many of his statements  that he values  a strong india u.s strategic partnership  so that is common to both  president trump and president biden now  i want to talk a little bit about the  quad  um and and the recent meeting there  you have australia japan united states  and india like-minded countries  their shared value of being democracies  i imagine that if you were sitting in  beijing it kind of looks like  encirclement  how would you respond to people who say  that  well um the court is  a grouping of countries which have us  which have the same vision  of a free open uh transparent  indo-pacific region  an international rules based order  international freedom navigation in in  global commons  and also issues such as open  connectivity  uh respecting the sovereignty and  territorial integrity of states  essentially working to bolster the the  capacities  and the abilities of countries in the  indo-pacific with asean as  its entirety in a manner that is both  constructive and cooperative  you mentioned open connectivity and  borders let's talk about borders first  and the last 24 hours there's been a  report in bloomberg about the fact that  some 300 square kilometers that used to  be  administered by india is now  administered by china and i'd be  interested in your response or comments  to that report clearly there we believe  that  you know the situation has involved  some level of deterioration in terms of  uh  it has contributed iteration in terms of  the ties between our two countries  because of the  unusual step of china to seek to  unilaterally alter the status quo you  have to keep in mind that there is no  common perception in the border  but yet if you you know seek to  change where the current you know  line where troops are then of course it  does impact on the larger relationship  we are concerned  that china has taken this step we are  very very clear that we will stand firm  and resolute  in our uh resolve not to allow our  territorial integrity or sovereignty to  be compromised  but i want to make sure do you confirm  or deny  the characterization from the report  that china is actually administrating  300 kilometers square kilometers more of  territory that they did not have at the  start of this year  well i'm not aware of where bloomberg is  getting its information from  but i can tell you that as far as we are  concerned uh says indian officials  familiar with the situation  you're asking me and i'm telling you  that as far as we are concerned we will  not  compromise on our territorial integrity  great i want to ask about another um  neighbor of yours  in terms of relationships up pakistan's  prime minister imran khan  recently called india a fascist state  inspired by the nazi regime this is what  he said  to a german news outlet what is your  response to that  well i can only say that is ripe coming  from the prime minister of a country  that does not recognize the state of  israel  that till now does not acknowledge that  the holocaust took place  that has provided a safe sanctuary for  osama bin laden  and mullah omar of the taliban years  after 9 11  a country that today has a debt which is  higher than his gdp  a country that has enormous  socio-economic problems and political  problems now with an  entire coalition of all the opposition  parties  seeking to unseat prime minister imran  khan  and those who back him from the deep  state and  it is clearly an attempt to distract  international attention uh and domestic  attention from his own problems  that iran khan is making this this  statement i mean i mean considering  his situation and pakistan's own  approach towards radicalism terrorism  and issues that are important to the  international community he really  shouldn't be making such  irresponsible statements  you'll allow me this question as someone  who's born in hong kong  you talk about rules based a lot of  rules can be interpreted to have been  broken in hong kong is this  a matter of concern for india  as as a matter of policy we don't  comment on on what  becomes the internal affairs of states  we have said that uh  there is a large community of indian  origin there's a large indian community  in hong kong so we're watching the  situation with  some level of attention and concern and  we will continue to do so  secretary shingla thank you very much  thank you so very much melissa  we have ananth krishnan in chennai  joining us to provide more context  following our interview with the foreign  secretary  he is the hindus china correspondent and  also the author of  india's china challenge ananth we just  heard the foreign secretary there  not really giving a clear response about  whether china really did  capture all that territory your thoughts  i think melissa it is understandable uh  for two reasons  i think uh the first is negotiations are  ongoing they've had seven  really unsuccessful rounds of talks at  the military level that have gone  nowhere  and i think that that's why they're  being a little iconic i think that the  second reason is it is a domestic  political issue in india and the modi  government has been coming under fire  from the opposition  for really not handling the china  problem at the border  so i think the government doesn't really  want to come out and say they've lost so  much territory but i think  uh from my own point of view melissa  right from the start i think  the government really could have been a  little bit more transparent and it could  have actually helped india dealing with  this  by showing to the world that it was  china that had occupied this territory  and  sought to redraw the line of actual  control and the government could have  actually publicly taken a stand and told  china listen these are the red lines  we have to return to status quo so i  think i find it a little puzzling  why the government's actually been so  reluctant to actually  spell out what actually the underground  situation is along the line of actual  control  and where china has made ingresses and  occupied territory  do you think there's a lack of trust on  the indian side when they look at the  activities of the chinese in the south  china sea  that they can never really speak to the  chinese and  come up with a resolution on the border  issue but unfortunately melissa that's  the only way i don't think  either country really wants a full-on  conflict  and as difficult as it is i think it's  going to be a combination of continuing  to talk at various levels between  military commanders between diplomats in  beijing and delhi  but at the same time keeping up a very  firm defensive posture as india has done  and an end august india actually  occupied several strategic heights  that have given it some bargaining power  in the negotiations that it did not have  previously so i think the hope is both  sides can find a meeting of minds but i  think  there is still some skepticism in india  with china after mobilizing all these  troops in early may  they really walk back from all the  territory they've gained i think that's  something that  is going to be very difficult for india  to achieve a full return to status quo  which is what india has publicly  demanded  well this is election week so i'm  curious do you agree with secretary  shringle's assessment  that the bilateral relationship between  india and the us  won't change regardless of who wins  either trump or biden  i would broadly agree that i think the  basic sort of uh  motivations of the india u.s  relationship won't change it's quite  interesting melissa that india is a bit  unique among many countries and actually  having quite a fond  sort of experience dealing with trump  over the last four years  there have been issues on trade i mean  who hasn't had issues with trump on  trade  uh but in terms of the defense  relationship in terms of the strategic  relationship  actually the india u.s relationship has  done fairly well under donald trump  and with the prime minister modi just  one evidence of that just last week week  before the election  you had secretary of state mike pompeo  and the defense secretary mark esper  come to delhi for talks just a week  before the election  i think the message really was from the  u.s that this is going to be a long-term  strategic relationship  and the fact that they signed uh the  fourth really important military defense  agreement  uh for sharing geospatial uh information  i think it really shows how even though  they aren't officially allies there is a  very very close defense partnership  that is growing even more close and  growing even more stronger  and regardless of whether it's trump or  biden in the white house i think that is  going to continue  there is going to be broad continuity  even if there will be some differences  i think one minor point of concern for  india is the democrat administration  tends to be more outspoken on issues  such as human rights  situation in kashmir something where  trump really didn't want to get involved  so i think the indian government is  aware there will be small changes but i  think there is a realization that  the broad controls of the relationship  really have a bipartisan resonance in  washington right now  i think that's a huge positive for the  india u.s relationship  and a sign of how much it's grown over  the last 10-15 years  where else do you see the us and india  working together  i think melissa one big gap is when it  comes to economic  cooperation i think that uh india us  australia and japan  the so-called quad have done quite a bit  in terms of security ties  they're going to be having huge  exercises the malabar naval exercises  this week  where really i think they have lagged  behind isn't actually coming up and  providing an alternative  to countries in the region in asia  when it comes to financing where they're  all reliant on chinese financing still  even though there has been some push  back to the belt and road initiative  we hear from countries such as sri lanka  for instance  where else can they go for funding there  really aren't many other sources besides  china and i think really other countries  should step up if they really want to  provide a credible  alternative to china in the region quite  frankly minnesota they haven't done  enough  anand krishnan thank you

